1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:12,800
Let's continue our discovery of caches and cover advanced cache design and cache performance.

2
00:00:12,800 --> 00:00:17,480
In the last lecture, we motivated the need for a memory hierarchy that consisted of one

3
00:00:17,480 --> 00:00:22,080
or more levels of caches between the processor pipeline and main memory.

4
00:00:22,080 --> 00:00:27,400
Here, I'm showing a two-level cache hierarchy integrated in our now-familiar five-stage

5
00:00:27,400 --> 00:00:30,560
pipeline processor.

6
00:00:30,560 --> 00:00:35,880
Separate L1 caches are typically used for instructions and data, since in any given

7
00:00:35,880 --> 00:00:40,360
clock cycle we would need to read from both simultaneously.

8
00:00:40,360 --> 00:00:46,760
The upper-level caches are typically unified, storing both instructions and data.

9
00:00:46,760 --> 00:00:51,600
The advantage of this organization is that while accessing main memory can be slow, if

10
00:00:51,600 --> 00:00:56,640
we keep most of the instructions and data we need, often a level or two closer to the

11
00:00:56,640 --> 00:01:06,400
processor, then we can minimize the number of full penalty trips to main memory.

12
00:01:06,400 --> 00:01:10,400
Last time, we discussed three key design considerations for caches.

13
00:01:10,400 --> 00:01:16,240
However, our discussion of placement and replacement were limited to direct mapped caches.

14
00:01:16,240 --> 00:01:25,440
In this lecture, we'll cover the more general concept of set associativity.

15
00:01:25,440 --> 00:01:30,680
The problem of cache placement is that the cache capacity is much smaller than main memory,

16
00:01:30,680 --> 00:01:35,320
so we need a smart way of choosing where to put blocks into the cache, such that if we

17
00:01:35,320 --> 00:01:40,960
tend to reuse blocks x and y often, we want to minimize the likelihood of x and y kicking

18
00:01:40,960 --> 00:01:43,280
each other out of the cache.

19
00:01:43,280 --> 00:01:49,200
Set associativity is the extent of flexibility that the cache has in terms of selecting where

20
00:01:49,200 --> 00:01:51,280
to place the block.

21
00:01:51,280 --> 00:01:53,560
Please note two new definitions.

22
00:01:53,560 --> 00:01:59,480
A cache way is one possible location in the cache that a given block can be placed.

23
00:01:59,480 --> 00:02:04,720
A cache set is a group of ways for a given block.

24
00:02:04,720 --> 00:02:11,200
A general n-way set associative cache is one where every single block has exactly n locations

25
00:02:11,200 --> 00:02:14,360
or n ways where it can be placed.

26
00:02:14,360 --> 00:02:18,640
At one extreme is a direct mapped cache that we discussed before.

27
00:02:18,640 --> 00:02:25,080
Here n is equal to 1, so every block has no choice but to go in its designated index.

28
00:02:25,080 --> 00:02:29,460
At the other extreme is a fully associative cache where n is equal to the total number

29
00:02:29,460 --> 00:02:31,800
of blocks that the cache can fit.

30
00:02:31,800 --> 00:02:39,960
In this case, every block can go anywhere in the cache.

31
00:02:39,960 --> 00:02:43,800
This is the standard structure of an n-way set associative cache.

32
00:02:43,800 --> 00:02:48,120
As before, the cache consists of a tag array and a data array.

33
00:02:48,120 --> 00:02:53,800
The difference now is that both arrays are composed of multiple sub arrays, one for each

34
00:02:53,800 --> 00:02:56,160
of the n-ways.

35
00:02:56,160 --> 00:03:02,000
To figure out which set of ways to use for a given block x, as before, we break it up

36
00:03:02,000 --> 00:03:08,920
into bits of its memory address, into a tag, an index, in this case a set index, and a

37
00:03:08,920 --> 00:03:11,120
block offset.

38
00:03:11,120 --> 00:03:15,480
This is the standard hash function used to index into a cache.

39
00:03:15,480 --> 00:03:20,560
The set bits select a specific index in each of the tags sub arrays.

40
00:03:20,560 --> 00:03:26,720
Upon a cache lookup, all n-ways are accessed in parallel, and each of the n tags are checked

41
00:03:26,720 --> 00:03:28,160
for a hit.

42
00:03:28,160 --> 00:03:34,840
In parallel to this, the data array is also accessed to retrieve the n-possible data blocks.

43
00:03:34,840 --> 00:03:39,320
If a tag hit is found, the corresponding block is selected and shifted based on the block

44
00:03:39,320 --> 00:03:40,320
offset.

45
00:03:41,080 --> 00:03:50,080
Finally, the data word for x is sent through the output port.

46
00:03:50,080 --> 00:03:56,360
Consider an example cache where memory addresses are 32 bits, blocks are 64 bytes in size,

47
00:03:56,360 --> 00:04:04,280
and the cache is 16-way set associative with a capacity of 128 kibabytes.

48
00:04:04,280 --> 00:04:10,000
Given our standard hash function, what is the minimum number of tag bits needed?

49
00:04:10,000 --> 00:04:11,520
Let's do some math.

50
00:04:11,520 --> 00:04:17,680
In a 128k cache with 64 bytes per block, we know that there's storage for 2048 blocks

51
00:04:17,680 --> 00:04:19,360
in this cache.

52
00:04:19,360 --> 00:04:24,960
But we have 16 ways, which mean we can hold 16 cache lines per set, so we further divide

53
00:04:24,960 --> 00:04:34,080
2048 by 16 and see that there are 128 sets.

54
00:04:34,080 --> 00:04:38,120
Now that we have the number of sets, we can figure out the number of bits in the tag,

55
00:04:38,120 --> 00:04:39,720
set, and offset.

56
00:04:39,720 --> 00:04:46,320
Log 64 means 6 bits for offset, log 128 means 7 bits for set, and subtracting those from

57
00:04:46,320 --> 00:04:54,000
the 32-bit address says we need 19 bits in the tag.

58
00:04:54,000 --> 00:04:57,320
We could choose a different hash function than what we've chosen, but there are two

59
00:04:57,320 --> 00:04:58,320
requirements.

60
00:04:58,320 --> 00:05:03,160
A block cannot map to multiple locations in the cache at once, and we must never wrongly

61
00:05:03,160 --> 00:05:07,240
think that block x is block y or vice versa.

62
00:05:07,240 --> 00:05:12,120
Every block must be uniquely identifiable.

63
00:05:12,120 --> 00:05:17,640
Okay, let's revisit cache replacement.

64
00:05:17,640 --> 00:05:22,320
As discussed last time, replacement in direct map caches is straightforward because each

65
00:05:22,320 --> 00:05:25,520
block only has one possible place it can go.

66
00:05:25,520 --> 00:05:30,640
If an address lookup goes to an occupied entry with a different tag, it's a miss and we

67
00:05:30,640 --> 00:05:32,400
